{"id": "00d906e945324ade9a40cda8adee36d9", "query": "Given n binary strings of length m, each represented as a binary vector or array of integers, and an array of size n of indices to remove, determine the median of the remaining binary strings after sorting in lexicographical order, where the number of remaining strings is 2^m - n, with time complexity O(n log n) due to sorting, and assuming 1 ≤ m ≤ 60 and 1 ≤ n ≤ min(2^m - 1, 100), and handling edge cases such as n = 1 or m = 1, and ensuring the solution can handle large n, specifically up to 1e6, and the median is defined as the string whose index is ⌊(2^m - n - 1)/2⌋?", "raw_text": " \n\n---------------------------------------------------\nYour answer:\n===START_JSON===\n{\n  \"explicit_dimensions\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"problem_input\",\n      \"value\": \"n arrays of length m of binary digits; n indices to remove; m between 1 and 60; 1 <= n <= min(2^m - 1, 100); up to 2^m - n strings remain after removal; target median is the lexicographically smallest k-th string among them.\",\n      \"justification\": \"The user describes the problem setting: n binary vectors, indices to remove, and constraints for n and m.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"time_complexity\",\n      \"value\": \"O(n log n)\",\n      \"justification\": \"Time constraint is O(n log n) due to sorting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"edge_cases\",\n      \"value\": \"handle n=1 and m=1; support large n up to 1e6; median definition via ⌊(2^m - n - 1)/2⌋\",\n      \"justification\": \"Edge cases and scalability constraints are explicitly mentioned.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missed_dimensions\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"binary_string_representation\",\n      \"value\": [\"bitvector\", \"array of integers\"],\n      \"justification\": \"The input representation is unclear; bitvectors vs integer arrays could affect parsing and storage efficiency.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"median_ties\",\n      \"value\": [\"lexicographic order; one winner per tie\"],\n      \"justification\": \"In case of ties at the median position, there might not be a single well-defined winner under lexicographic ordering; one possible convention is to choose the leftmost among tied candidates.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"string_length_consistency\",\n      \"value\": [\"m consistent across all strings\"],\n      \"justification\": \"Strings should have the same length m; if lengths vary, median calculation is ill-defined.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"sorting_stability\",\n      \"value\": [\"stable sort needed to preserve equal strings' relative order\"],\n      \"justification\": \"Lexicographic order implies a stable sort to ensure correct ordering when multiple strings compare equal.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"remaining_string_count\",\n      \"value\": \"2^m - n\",\n      \"justification\": \"Exact count of remaining strings depends on m and n; this influences algorithm design and memory usage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"median_index_definition\",\n      \"value\": \"⌊(2^m - n - 1)/2⌋\",\n      \"justification\": \"The exact formula for the target index of the median string needs careful consideration given the binomial distribution of removed bits.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"bit-level_median\",\n      \"value\": \"binary digit median across positions rather than lexicographic string median\",\n      \"justification\": \"An alternative interpretation is to compute the median bit value across all positions, which could yield different results depending on the definition of'median'.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n===END_JSON===\n\nPlease verify the accuracy of your output before submitting it. This output was generated using the described guidelines. If any part seems incorrect, please let me know. I'll review again.", "json_data": {"explicit_dimensions": [{"name": "problem_input", "value": "n arrays of length m of binary digits; n indices to remove; m between 1 and 60; 1 <= n <= min(2^m - 1, 100); up to 2^m - n strings remain after removal; target median is the lexicographically smallest k-th string among them.", "justification": "The user describes the problem setting: n binary vectors, indices to remove, and constraints for n and m."}, {"name": "time_complexity", "value": "O(n log n)", "justification": "Time constraint is O(n log n) due to sorting."}, {"name": "edge_cases", "value": "handle n=1 and m=1; support large n up to 1e6; median definition via ⌊(2^m - n - 1)/2⌋", "justification": "Edge cases and scalability constraints are explicitly mentioned."}], "implicit_dimensions": [{"name": "binary_string_representation", "value": ["bitvector", "array of integers"], "justification": "The input representation is unclear; bitvectors vs integer arrays could affect parsing and storage efficiency."}, {"name": "median_ties", "value": ["lexicographic order; one winner per tie"], "justification": "In case of ties at the median position, there might not be a single well-defined winner under lexicographic ordering; one possible convention is to choose the leftmost among tied candidates."}, {"name": "string_length_consistency", "value": ["m consistent across all strings"], "justification": "Strings should have the same length m; if lengths vary, median calculation is ill-defined."}, {"name": "sorting_stability", "value": ["stable sort needed to preserve equal strings' relative order"], "justification": "Lexicographic order implies a stable sort to ensure correct ordering when multiple strings compare equal."}, {"name": "remaining_string_count", "value": "2^m - n", "justification": "Exact count of remaining strings depends on m and n; this influences algorithm design and memory usage."}, {"name": "median_index_definition", "value": "⌊(2^m - n - 1)/2⌋", "justification": "The exact formula for the target index of the median string needs careful consideration given the binomial distribution of removed bits."}, {"name": "bit-level_median", "value": "binary digit m